{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אלף למטה אלף למטה", :translit "elef lamatteh elef lamatteh", :gloss "a thousand per tribe, a thousand per tribe"} {:he "כ\"ד אלפים", :translit "k'd arbayim", :gloss "twenty-four thousand"} {:he "כ\"ד אלפים שהפילו מישדאל", :translit "k'd arbayim shehipilu miyisrael", :gloss "twenty-four thousand who fell of Israel"} {:he "לב א' לאביהם שבשמים", :translit "lev ech la'avihem shebashamayim", :gloss "one heart to their Father in heaven"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Verse begins and ends with the letter alef, signaling unity of heart"}], :frame {:text "After Israel sinned with the daughters of Moab, a plague struck the people and twenty-four thousand died (Numbers 25:9). The verse specifies that the one who was killed was Zimri son of Salu (Numbers 25:14). The midrashic tradition that Zimri's Hebrew name was Shalum son of Zuar, leader of the tribe of Simeon, appears in Tanchuma. The phrase 'one heart to their Father in heaven' echoes the rabbinic description of Israel at Sinai in Shabbat 88a as standing 'as one person with one heart' (k'ish echad b'lev echad).", :sources ["Numbers 25:9" "Numbers 25:14" "Shabbat 88a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Verse begins and ends with the letter alef, signaling unity of heart", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:32:09.694044332Z", :model "glm-5.2"}